title: The Railway Strike
subtitle: The trains stop, and the President bans the strike.
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1922 and month = 2
max-visits: 1

= The Railway Strike

On 1 February the Reich railway officials walk out. Their salaries are worth half of what they were a year ago. Within a day almost nothing moves between Hamburg and Munich, and President Ebert signs an emergency decree forbidding civil servants to strike. Strikers are threatened with dismissal and prison.

In Berlin the municipal workers come out in sympathy: gas, water and the electric trams stop. The union federation, the ADGB, keeps its distance. The railway union is not one of its own, and its leaders fear a general strike they cannot control.

The strikers are not communists. Most of them voted for the Centre or the SPD. But they are fighting the Republic's first emergency decree against strikers.

- @solidarity: Call solidarity strikes in the big works.
- @unions: Press the ADGB to back the strikers, inside the unions.
- @general: Call for a general strike against the decree.
- @quiet: Keep our members out of it.

@solidarity
subtitle: [? if lock_councils = 1 : Against the party line: costs Unity. ?] Our factory cells walk out. Strong for a week, then lonely.
choose-if: lock_councils < 2
unavailable-subtitle: The [+ leading_faction_adj +] line forbids it.
on-arrival: {!
if (Q.lock_councils == 1) {
    Q.unity -= 10;
    Q[Q.leading_faction + '_satisfaction'] -= 10;
}
Q.e22_railway = 1;
Q.militancy += 6;
Q.worker_support += 3;
Q.repression += 4;
Q.offensive_satisfaction += 4;
Q.lean_offensive += 1;
Q.district_berlin_loyalty += 4;
!}

In Berlin, Halle and the Ruhr our cells bring out a few thousand workers. On 7 February the railway officials' leaders settle. Our strikers go back to work with nothing, but they are remembered.

@unions
subtitle: [? if lock_union_work = 1 : Against the party line: costs Unity. ?] The Right's way. Slow, and it builds our standing in the unions.
choose-if: lock_union_work < 2
unavailable-subtitle: The [+ leading_faction_adj +] line forbids work inside the reformist unions.
on-arrival: {!
if (Q.lock_union_work == 1) {
    Q.unity -= 10;
    Q[Q.leading_faction + '_satisfaction'] -= 10;
}
Q.e22_railway = 2;
Q.worker_support += 4;
Q.spd_relation += 3;
Q.unionist_satisfaction += 6;
Q.lean_unionist += 2;
!}

Our members move resolutions in a hundred union branches: the federation must defend the right to strike. The ADGB does not move, but its members have seen who stood up for the railwaymen.

@general
subtitle: The Left's call. Brave, and few will follow it.
view-if: offensive_gone = 0 or ultraleft_gone = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.e22_railway = 3;
Q.militancy += 10;
Q.repression += 8;
Q.worker_support -= 1;
Q.offensive_satisfaction += 6;
Q.ultraleft_satisfaction += 4;
Q.unionist_satisfaction -= 6;
Q.lean_offensive += 2;
Q.spd_relation -= 4;
!}

*Die Rote Fahne* calls for a general strike to tear up the decree. It does not come. The police raid our offices in three cities, and the strike collapses on 7 February.

@quiet
subtitle: The party avoids trouble, and the strikers notice.
on-arrival: {!
Q.e22_railway = 0;
Q.worker_support -= 3;
Q.militancy -= 4;
Q.offensive_satisfaction -= 5;
Q.luxemburgist_satisfaction += 2;
!}

The Zentrale publishes a statement of sympathy and nothing more. The strike ends after a week, with a few concessions and many dismissals.
